"""Describes each customizable gene byte in an El-Fish .ROE file: which byte it lives at, what it
changes, and the value range each slider is allowed to use.

This data mirrors the names, descriptions, and section order of the companion web guide
(El-Fish Fish Customisation Guide) exactly -- when that guide is edited, re-run the sync so
this file stays in lockstep: same bytes, same order, same section/group structure, same
labels and descriptions. A byte the guide drops should be dropped from here too.

A handful of bytes do double duty and appear TWICE in the guide, under two different
headings -- most often a Shape slider that also fully disables the fin at value 0. The guide
is the source of truth, so each shows up here as two separate entries too (two separate rows
in the UI), one per offset value -- NOT merged into one. Moving either one's slider updates
the other automatically at runtime, since they're the same underlying byte; each entry's
`changes` text ends with an auto-generated note pointing at where its sibling entry lives.
Only genuinely identical duplicate rows (the guide repeating the exact same row twice, a
copy/paste slip) are collapsed down to one.

safe_min/safe_max are each slider's actual min/max -- most genes stop well short of the guide's
declared range because the picture stops changing (or the game destabilizes) before the byte's
theoretical max, so the slider simply doesn't go there; these come from pixel-diffing the
bundled preview images, not from the guide's text. `safe_note` IS the guide's own declared
"Value Range" text verbatim, shown to the user as-is even where it's wider than the slider.

`section` is "Fins", "Body", or "Other" -- the top-level tab a gene lives under, matching the
guide's <h2> headings. `subsection` is the guide's <h3> heading within a section (e.g. "Shape",
"Colour", "Pattern", "Enable/Disable" under Fins) or None where the guide has no such heading
(Body and Other). `group` is the guide's collapsible sub-heading (e.g. "Dorsal fin", "Body
Shape", "Eyes") -- the finest grouping level, always present.
"""

